over the past week I've spent a dozen  hours with warframe or with the new apex  legends and five in anthems open demo  period and as the week ended a question  struck me how long can this games as  service trend continued as entries in a  prevalent sub-genre these life style  games rely on players returning to them  as often as possible there are only as  good as their long-term cash-spending  player base overwatch does this through  holiday events destiny does it with  major expansions warframe hooks its  players with a drip feed of character  progression asking them to farm  resources across an array of complicated  systems for many players this model is  ideal rather than spending $60 on dozens  of games each year they can instead  focus their time and money on one or two  of them they can master their mechanics  and ideas catching up with friends on a  set schedule based around a weekly  rendezvous they can devote their time to  fewer virtual worlds but at the heart of  this development model is an inherent  problem as each new lifestyle game  releases the genre cannibalizes itself  players either move from one platform to  another or remain loyal to the games  they already know hear time is the most  valued quantity and eventually there  won't be enough to go around now this  problem isn't entirely new video game  developers have always competed for  players time and money with robust  feature lists and attractive box art  shooters introduced new maps MMO xanex  their existing worlds single-player  adventures paced their stories to keep  players engaged until the end only to  introduce a new game plus mode upon  completion theoretically this lends more  value to the players purchase and of  course value is always subjective but in  the case of lifestyle games the question  of value becomes even more complicated  this is due in large part to the notion  of an end game developers often consign  their best ideas to these later stages  from Destiny's explained raids to the  divisions cutthroat dark zones the mere  act of getting to these features could  take dozens if not hundreds of hours  only then can you see the fruits of your  labor and witness the full potential  value  of each game and of course barring any  prisoner of azkaban hourglass  shenanigans seeing the entirety of each  of these games is literally impossible  you almost have to decide where your  loyalties lie that's why this  development model is becoming  increasingly risky more so than any  other genre this one relies on player's  loyalty to be successful if a studio can  attract new players hook them and keep  them entertained it can recoup its money  with each new update expansion or micro  transaction so when I see Bioware a  company long known for its single-player  RPGs trying to break into the sphere I  can't help but worry as the release  approaches it's entering an arena that's  already low on real estate its competing  with the likes of fortnight destiny to  black ops or warframe Rainbow six siege  for honor apex legends monster hunter  world and even sea of thieves which has  seen a massive recent boost thanks to  prominent twitch players and of course  there's the elephant in the room  the division 2 which releases exactly  one month after anthem developer massive  entertainment has the clear advantage  here it has 3 years of experience with  the previous division to draw from it  has an avid existing fanbase following  every news story every update out of the  studio and its publisher Ubisoft if I'm  Bioware then this is the last game I  want breathing down my neck you might  look at anthem in the division 2 and  think they're completely different games  that wouldn't compete but they rely on  the same survival techniques  it's no secret that Bioware is a shadow  of its former self any RPG fan would  probably echo the sentiment even before  Mass Effect Andromeda is released with  its poor critical reception and  tumultuous launch the studio lost many  of its veteran members  what's more Andromeda's creator Bioware  Montreal was then absorbed into its  sister studio EA motive in the aftermath  from the outside looking in it seems as  if Bioware's very survival relies on  anthem being the next big thing a recent  report on Kotaku reinforces this notion  news editor Jason Schreyer spoke to  numerous Bioware employees who think the  studio's future is inextricably tied to  anthem  this comes after news that mark daraa  executive producer of the Dragon Age  franchise which has a sequel in some  stage of the  Elliman was moved to the anthem team  with Bioware Edmonton and bio are Austin  completely focused on the loot shooter  it's clear the developer is betting most  of its chips on anthem being a hit to be  clear I'm all for competition fueling  creativity in any video game genre and  from what at played of anthem during its  pre-release period I've had a lot of fun  despite how repetitive it can be I  thoroughly enjoy how smoothly it plays  and I can't wait to see the final  product yet as I said before this genre  relies on more than just good mechanics  and with the division - on the way and a  plethora of other lifestyle games still  going strong I can't help but see  Bioware as David facing off against a  dozen goliaths but of course if history  tells us anything about that age-old  story it's this the outcome is never set  in stone  [Music]
 

